[Music]  [Music]  the austro-hungarian Navy was oftentimes  overlooked in history as a meager  attempt of trying to catch up to the  rest of the European powers Howard this  view is an entirely correct as the  Imperial Navy was far from small and at  one point was the seventh largest Navy  in the world plus the technology used on  its ships like radios was far from  outdated and the naval structure based  off of the British Navy was on par with  every other superpower of the early 20th  century so with all this said why people  today don't know much about the  Australian Navy let alone know that  austria-hungary even had a Navy this is  mostly due to the fact that even though  the austro-hungarian Navy was strong in  its own right it was always treated as  an afterthought in the empire this can  be seen through the fact that the Navy  was part of the war ministry which was  controlled by the military as such the  naval budget was very small numbering  only around 18 million crowns in 1899  this was five times less compared to the  Italian naval budget and 30 and a half  times less compared to the British naval  budget Howard this naval budget would  slowly start to increase going into  World War one not only because of the  looming threat of war but also due to  the fact that Archduke Franz Ferdinand  was very keen on having a large and  strong Navy as he saw the benefits such  naval power can bring to the country he  heavily campaigned on the side of the  Navy and ironically it could be said  that without him the impale Navy  wouldn't even be ready for World War one  but the lack of money was far from the  only problem the austro-hungarian Navy  had to face for one the multicultural  nature of the environment that you could  hear eight different languages or more  spoken on any given ship and officers  were required to know at least four of  these languages naturally such structure  didn't always result in the most  smoothest of order executions when he  came to the sailors themself majority of  them in the 19th century came from  Croatia and Italy as they were largely  the only ethnicities in the empire that  had any extensive sailing experience  however this started to change in the  early 20th century when the involvement  of minorities became more prominent  across the Empire and the naval  recruitment and training became more  standardized with this increased  recruitment of different ethnic groups  to avoid any confusion on a ship people  have the same / similar ethnicities  where oftentimes given similar roles so  they could communicate orders more  easily ie Germans Czechs and Slovaks  where Gunners  machinist Hungarians were anti-air and  machine-gun crew Croats and Slovenes  were deckhands and Stoker's etc lastly  probably the most major problem facing  the Australian Navy was in the  construction of its ships at first the  construction was limited due to the lack  of Hungarian parliamentary support who  viewed the Navy as a pointless expensive  waste of money however as an all-out war  started to be more and more inevitable  they backed down with their opposition  but with that another major problem  started to come to light which was the  fact that the double nature of the  monarchy manned that all governmental  industrial spending had to be divided  between the hungarian part and austrian  part 34.4% to sixty five point six  percent respectively with that said the  34.4% still proved problematic for the  navy to spend in hungary as majority of  the austro-hungarian industry was in the  austrian half of the empire and whatever  little industry was in the hungarian  part wasn't up to date to be able to  build the high-tech ships that the  Imperial Navy had designed so the ship  designs had to be altered for the  Hungarian industry to be able to build  the 34.4% of the ships most notable of  these changes was the use of less  powered engines different electrical  wiring different boilers the use of  different screws and bolts etc these  ships would later improve not as  reliable as the ones built in austrian  part of the empire however what was  remarkable was that even du told these  changes the ships built in the Hungarian  part weren't far behind and benchmarks  compared to the austrian ships with all  that said the limited governmental  funding and the division of construction  between the two parts of the empire  resulted in the fact that the  austro-hungarian navy had to very  heavily budget its operations to remain  relevant on the world stage  such budgeting included restricted use  of high class ships lower number of  active operational squadrons building  ships with a lower overall tonnage and  mostly inclusive lower wages compared to  its rivals this budgeting proved to be  quite effective as at the outbreak of  World War one austria-hungary had 110  ships and five submarines with three  extra dreadknots in construction in  comparison the Italian Navy which was  viewed as the main rival of the Imperial  Navy had only hundred and forty-nine  ships with twenty-one submarines and no  boats and active construction and even  though it may be more compared to what  the Imperial Navy had considering how  much of a larger budget  Navy had to work with it wasn't that big  of a difference therefore going into  World War one the austro-hungarian Navy  was far from being a pushover and the  Allies had to seriously consider the  Imperial Navy strength especially when  it came to planning Mediterranean  operations speaking of which when it  came to preparing for World War one the  Imperial Navy had three battle  strategies in mind the first was  conceived prior to the breakup of the  Triple Alliance when Italy was still  considered a viable ally this plan  involved all Hungarian Navy and the  Italian Navy to use Italian bases in  Sicily to block the French but mainly  the British from crossing the  Mediterranean and consequently the Suez  Canal this would mean that Britain would  be cut off from its fast route into the  Eastern colonies and would have to use  the long way around which would  drastically diminish its fighting power  especially when it came to a drawn-out  war however the strategy was far from  foolproof and both the Italian and  Imperial navies had their doubts about  being able to keep the French and  British navies from crossing the  Mediterranean as such the back-up plan  was to use the bases in Sicily to  heavily harass Allied shipping in the  area so that it proved too costly for  the Allies to use those shipping routes  however this entire naval plan fell  through when it became increasingly  clear that Italy wasn't going to follow  through on its promise to the Triple  Alliance as such the second plan of the  Hungarian Navy was to do a preemptive  strike on the main Italian ports and the  Adriatic effectively crippling the  Italian ability to the naval operations  in the area and enabling of the Imperial  Navy to take full control of that part  of the Mediterranean third and also the  most conservative plan of the  austro-hungarian Navy was to use its  vast number of torpedo boats to focus on  the defense of the Adriatic coastline  protecting the valuable ports towns and  shipping all while threatening to attack  with the rest of its Navy at any moment  this way they could keep the much larger  French and Italian Mediterranean navies  in check effectively hindering their  actions in other theatres of the war  when the grade were started in 1914 the  initial neutrality of Italy meant that  the Imperial Navy had to implement their  defensive tactic as they couldn't  execute their preemptive bombardment of  the Italian Adriatic coastline when  Italy finally joined the war in 1915 the  Imperial Navy executed their second  Howard is proved ineffective as their  preemptive strike / not so pre-emptive  as Italy had ample time to prepare for  it with that the Imperial Navy went back  to their defensive strategy which proved  effective because the Allied navies  didn't wanted to risk a full-on naval  battle with them resorting to small  battle missions and bulk aides of  daughter on toast rates this meant that  there were continuous small skirmishes /  battles throughout the Adriatic like the  Battle of those Anto Straits where the  Imperial Navy managed to score a victory  but that battle just as every other  Imperial naval battle didn't result in  any decisive changes so the  austro-hungarian Navy excluding the  submarines was forced to operate only in  the Adriatic the stagnation combined  with the dwindling close deployment that  the austro-hungarian Navy didn't go out  with a bang but with a fizzle keeping  the Allied armies at bay in the Adriatic  but remaining largely stagnant till the  end of the war after which it got  divided between the Allies and the newly  formed ego Slavia and then mostly  dismantled for scrap and with that the  austro-hungarian Navy which was able to  hold its own against the Allied navies  got mostly forgotten remaining today  largely as a small footnote when talking  about the austro-hungarian Empire this  video is part of a much larger YouTube  naval history collaboration codenamed  Operation Odysseus involving 17  different history YouTube channels out  of these youtubers potential history and  wartime history have video set in the  20th century just as mine was so check  them out and if you want to see all the  operational Decius videos there's a link  to a playlist down in the description  mean cookie dough also have a youtube  history podcast you can check out and my  name is advisor  see you next time

